course: dictionary
lesson: 1813
title: Finding clues
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A clue is a piece of information that helps you solve a mystery, a puzzle or a problem. A detective looks for clues, and in a crossword a clue is a hint that helps you fill in a word. When you read, the words around a new word can be clues to what it means too.
explain: «Three clues, one happy answer.» Here the clues point to a surprise being planned, so you know something is coming, and it is kinder not to go looking for it.
explain: «AI can make pictures of real people doing things they never did.» Strange hands, blurry writing and odd backgrounds are clues that a picture may be fake. But clues will not always work, so look at more than one before you decide.
explain: «A school legend is usually told about a kid at my cousin's school.» That friend-of-a-friend detail is a clue that the story may be made up, because you cannot ask that kid whether it is true.
fact: A clue is a piece of information that helps you solve a mystery, puzzle or problem.
fact: A clue can also be a hint given in a puzzle or a crossword.
fact: «Not have a clue» means you do not know anything about something.
fact: To look for clues means to search carefully for information that helps you find an answer.
fact: A clue is a hint, not always proof, so check more than one clue before you decide.
exercise: «Three clues, one happy answer» tells you a surprise is being planned. What do the clues tell you? | Someone is lost in the house | *There is a surprise coming | The party has been cancelled
exercise: Ella says, «I do not have a clue what this puzzle means.» What does not have a clue mean? | She has lost her pencil | She has solved the puzzle very quickly | *She does not know anything about it
exercise: In a crossword, what is a clue? | The end of the game | A picture of the puzzle | *A hint that helps you fill in a word
test: A detective searches the room for ____ that help solve the mystery. Which word fits the gap? | lunches | puddles | holidays | *clues
test: «The strange hands and blurry writing in the picture are clues, but clues will not always work.» What does this tell you? | *A clue can help, but it is not always proof | Blurry writing is never a clue | Clues are always right
exam: «The story is about a kid at my cousin's school, and nobody can ask him.» Why is this a clue that it may be made up? | Because it is a happy story | Because it has a cousin in it | Because it is a long story | *Because nobody can check it with the person in it
